# class List extends Array { constructor() { super() } }; new List().push(1)
# An instance of the class may be no array with pristine storage, so the overload for array-likes is in play too: it converts, the conversion finds Array.prototype.toString, and that calls `join` without operands.
# Nothing that the host makes is passed, so its start-up modules stay out
